As expected, based on the theoretical model on which the instrument is based, the domains are correlated with each other and, no, neither theory nor statistical tests suggest that any should be collapsed.
Factor correlations:
       resDom proDom intDom actDom socDom heaDom disDom
resDom  1.000
proDom  0.764  1.000
intDom  0.617  0.674  1.000
actDom  0.678  0.676  0.752  1.000
socDom  0.781  0.744  0.753  0.783  1.000
heaDom  0.585  0.696  0.833  0.858  0.739  1.000
disDom  0.625  0.596  0.496  0.590  0.542  0.511

The M2 fit statistics for this model, with the possible exception of the RMSEA, and the SRMSR in particular, identify potential concerns with model fit:

M2(model, type = "M2*", QMC=T, calcNull = T):
            M2  df p      RMSEA    RMSEA_5  RMSEA_95    SRMSR     TLI       CFI
stats 2690.236 429 0 0.07192124 0.06929696 0.0745002 0.339581 0.73082 0.7623905

These fit statistics are poor compared to the corresponding model in Lavaan (which treated the items as ordinal, and was estimated with DWLS): RMSEA=0.057 (90% CI 0.055–0.060), SRMR= 0.039, CFI=0.951. While I don’t expect them to be the same, such a large discrepancy is surprising.

To see why the mirt model is fitting poorly, I obtained the residual matrix used to compute the SRMSR statistic, part of which I have pasted below.  All the residuals between items within each domain are small, while the residuals for items between domains are large.

This leads me to wonder if the estimated covariances between domains are being adequately incorporated into the computation of the SRMSR, TLI and CFI?

